WEBVTT

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:10.510
<v Ira Glass>OK, here's what intrigued Ben. His friend Sonari had told him that he'd come up with this thing. And there's no way to say this without sounding like an infomercial, but Sonari was doing this thing that was saving him lots of money.
<v Ira Glass>好的，Ben对此很感兴趣。他的朋友Sonari告诉他，他想出了一个点子。如果不说得像电视购物广告那样听起来太夸张，Sonari确实发现了一个能为他省下不少钱的方法。

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:18.130
<v Ira Glass>And Sonari is exactly the kind of person who would know about this kind of thing. He's a business reporter. He's one of our colleagues at NPR News. Anyway, here's Ben.
<v Ira Glass>Sonari正是那种会关注此类事情的人。他是一位商业记者。他是我们在NPR新闻部的同事之一。总之，这是Ben的故事。

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:26.750
<v Ben Calhoun>He told me he was just saving on everything. Like he used it for shoes, for clothes, big stuff like new tires.
<v Ben Calhoun>他告诉我，他几乎在所有事情上都能省钱。比如他买鞋、买衣服，还有像换新轮胎这样的大件物品。

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:36.970
<v Ira Glass>Ben, by the way, is This American Life producer Ben Calhoun. He and Sonari are buddies, and Ben says that Sonari came up with this new financial strategy one day when Sonari was shopping for shoes.
<v Ira Glass>顺便提一下，Ben是《This American Life》的制作人Ben Calhoun。他和Sonari是好友，Ben说Sonari是在某天买鞋时偶然想出这个财务策略的。

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.520
<v Ben Calhoun>And he realized that all the shoes that he wanted were the super expensive shoes.
<v Ben Calhoun>他意识到，他看中的所有鞋子都是那种超级昂贵的鞋。

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.150
<v Ira Glass>Oh.
<v Ira Glass>噢。

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:52.680
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah. Yeah. And he keeps coming back to this pair that's way too expensive. And he stays there for hours stressing about this decision, until finally he caved.
<v Ben Calhoun>对，没错。他总是对那双贵得离谱的鞋念念不忘。他在那里待了好几个小时，为这个决定感到苦恼，直到最后他妥协了。

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.530
<v Ben Calhoun>So he's like, I'm just gonna do it. I'm gonna do it. I'm gonna do it. So he got up to the counter, and this is what he says happened.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以他想，我一定要买下它。我一定要买。我一定要买。于是他走到柜台前，这就是他所说的当时发生的事情。

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:04.780
<v Sonari Glinton>I remembered this thing a guy that I'd interviewed had talked about, about the good guy discount.
<v Sonari Glinton>我记起曾经采访过的一个人提到过的事情，关于“好人折扣”。

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.720
<v Ira Glass>The good guy discount?
<v Ira Glass>“好人折扣”？

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:16.720
<v Ben Calhoun>The good guy discount. This is the thing. Sonari, he'd interviewed this negotiations expert from Columbia University Business School.
<v Ben Calhoun>“好人折扣”。事情就是这样。Sonari曾采访过哥伦比亚大学商学院的一位谈判专家。

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:28.560
<v Ben Calhoun>And the guy told him about this technique where you say, can I get a good guy discount on that? You're a good guy. I'm a good guy. Come on, just a good guy discount.
<v Ben Calhoun>那个人告诉他一种技巧，你可以说：“我能享受‘好人折扣’吗？你是好人，我也是好人。拜托，就给个‘好人折扣’吧。”

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.790
<v Ira Glass>And this works for the professor?
<v Ira Glass>这对那位教授真的管用吗？

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.860
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah. It's supposed to be like a thing.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的。这据称是一种招数。

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.230
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:40.290
<v Ben Calhoun>So Sonari remembered this when he was about to buy these shoes. And honestly, he didn't think it was going to work.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以当Sonari准备买鞋时，他想起了这一点。老实说，他没觉得会成功。

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:56.360
<v Sonari Glinton>And I go, hey you know, is there a good guy discount? And he goes, what? It's like, you know, you've seen me here all day. You know I want these shoes. It's tough for me, blah, blah, blah.
<v Sonari Glinton>我说：“嘿，你知道吗，这鞋能打‘好人折扣’吗？”他说：“什么？”我说：“你知道的，你看着我在这里逛了一整天了。你知道我想要这双鞋。对我来说这很难，诸如此类。”

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:02.300
<v Sonari Glinton>And he looks at me and he goes, I tell you what, brother. And he swiped the card--
<v Sonari Glinton>他看着我，然后说：“兄弟，我跟你说吧。”然后他刷了卡——

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.510
<v Ben Calhoun>Like his little authorization card?
<v Ben Calhoun>就像他的那种小额授权卡？

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:09.770
<v Sonari Glinton>Yeah. And he goes, I'll give you 25% off.
<v Sonari Glinton>对。他说：“我给你打七五折。”

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:11.730
<v Ira Glass>25% off!
<v Ira Glass>七五折！

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:12.480
<v Ben Calhoun>Mm hmm.
<v Ben Calhoun>嗯。

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.300
<v Sonari Glinton>[LAUGHING] It was the most positive reinforcement you could ever get.
<v Sonari Glinton>[笑] 这简直是你所能得到的最棒的正面反馈了。

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.100
<v Ben Calhoun>[LAUGHS]
<v Ben Calhoun>[笑]

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:24.020
<v Sonari Glinton>Like, I was so happy.
<v Sonari Glinton>当时我简直太开心了。

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:34.020
<v Ira Glass>So Sonari told Ben all this because Sonari had started doing this all the time, asking for the good guy discount. And he thought that Ben should try it too. And Ben suggested this might be a good radio story for our show.
<v Ira Glass>Sonari把这一切都告诉了Ben，因为他已经开始频繁使用这一招，到处询问“好人折扣”。他觉得Ben也应该试试。Ben提出，这或许是我们节目的一段不错的广播素材。

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:50.270
<v Ira Glass>And all of us here at the ratio show, we all said great. And we all told him, of course, as part of the story you're gonna have to go out and try to get the discount yourself. And Ben turned red and started to squirm, but he said sure.
<v Ira Glass>我们广播节目组的所有人都觉得太棒了。当然，我们也告诉他，作为故事的一部分，他必须亲自去尝试争取折扣。Ben脸涨得通红，开始有些坐立不安，但他还是答应了。

00:02:50.270 --> 00:03:04.770
<v Ira Glass>And we put it into our story list, and it sat there. And a month went by, and then a second month, and a third-- six months, nine months, 11 months.
<v Ira Glass>我们把它写进了选题单，然后就搁置了。一个月过去了，接着是第二个月、第三个月……六个月，九个月，11个月。

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:19.090
<v Ira Glass>And now and then, one of us would remember this at a story meeting and say, whatever happened to that good guy discount thing? And Ben would get all embarrassed. And it was clear he was just hating the idea that he was  going to actually have to go out in the world and do this thing. He could not bring himself to.
<v Ira Glass>偶尔，我们会在选题会上想起这件事，问道：“那个‘好人折扣’的事情怎么样了？”Ben就会感到非常尴尬。很明显，他非常抵触这个主意，因为他真的得走到现实世界中去执行这件事。他就是过不了自己那一关。

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:25.390
<v Ben Calhoun>I just could not get over the pitch itself. It's so cheesy.
<v Ben Calhoun>我就是无法克服这个推销话术本身。它太烂俗了。

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.780
<v Ira Glass>To say, I'm a good guy, you're a good guy.
<v Ira Glass>说“我是好人，你是好人”。

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:41.170
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah. I think it's kinda smarmy. Like, I love Sonari. I love Sonari. I think that Sonari is a wonderful person. But it traffics in this term of good guy when it's nonsense.
<v Ben Calhoun>没错。我觉得这有点虚伪。我爱Sonari，真的。我认为Sonari是个很棒的人。但当他拿“好人”这个词来作为一种筹码时，简直荒谬。

00:03:41.170 --> 00:04:15.280
<v Ben Calhoun>It's me saying, I'm a good guy, which I feel like it's the kind of thing of saying like, I'm so humble. The second part of it is you're saying, and the thing I'm going to do as a good guy is I'm going to ask you to do me a favor and cost yourself money. That's what a good guy I am. And I don't know. I find it to be not the behavior of a good guy.
<v Ben Calhoun>这就好比我在说“我是个好人”，这感觉就像是在说“我是多么谦逊”一样。第二部分是你还在说：“作为好人，我要请你帮个忙，让你自己损失点钱。”这算哪门子好人啊？我不确定，我觉得这根本不是一个好人的行为。

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:24.270
<v Ira Glass>A good guy, Ben said, would not make somebody else uncomfortable on purpose. And he was convinced that asking for a good guy discount, it puts the sales person on the spot.
<v Ira Glass>Ben说，一个好人不会故意让别人感到难堪。他坚信，询问“好人折扣”会让销售人员陷入尴尬。

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:34.310
<v Ben Calhoun>You're asking them to break the rules for you for absolutely no reason. And I hate making other people feel uncomfortable.
<v Ben Calhoun>你是在要求他们无缘无故地为你破坏规则。我讨厌让别人感到不舒服。

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:38.340
<v Ira Glass>I have a different take on it than you do. I think it shows moxie.
<v Ira Glass>我和你的看法不同。我认为这展现了勇气。

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.980
<v Ben Calhoun>[LAUGHS]
<v Ben Calhoun>[笑]

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:53.670
<v Ira Glass>And the good guy discount is part of what makes this country great, even if Sonari's the only one who's doing it. That impulse of just like, I'm just going to go for it. I'm going to say something that's ridiculous and corny and just see if it works.
<v Ira Glass>而且“好人折扣”是让这个国家变得伟大的原因之一，即使Sonari是唯一一个这么做的人。那种“我就是要去试一试”的冲动。我说出一些荒谬和陈词滥调的话，只是想看看是否奏效。

00:04:53.670 --> 00:05:01.150
<v Ira Glass>I mean, you know, in the movies, that's what gets the girl. In the movies, that's what makes you a hero.
<v Ira Glass>我是说，在电影里，这可是能追到女孩的招数。在电影里，这能让你成为英雄。

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:06.200
<v Ben Calhoun>If I were to try this three times, how many times do you think it would work?
<v Ben Calhoun>如果我试三次，你觉得能成功几次？

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.870
<v Ira Glass>One.
<v Ira Glass>一次。

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:19.060
<v Ben Calhoun>So here's one question before I go in. Is it important that I do the whole, I'm a good guy, you're a good guy?
<v Ben Calhoun>好吧，在我出发前还有一个问题。我必须把整套说辞——“我是好人，你是好人”——都说出来吗？

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.890
<v Ira Glass>You gotta say something. What else are you gonna say?
<v Ira Glass>你得说点什么。不然你还能说什么？

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:55.920
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio program, good guys. We have four stories including I go with Ben as he tries to find out whether or not he has what it takes to get free stuff by claiming to be a good guy, even though he worries that doing that, trying the entire exercise, means that he is not a good guy at all, because friends, it is a struggle sometimes to know what a good guy would and would not do. And in each of our stories today, we have guys trying to find that line, trying to stay on one side of the line, trying to stay good guys in a world where we all know, that can be very, very difficult.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的广播节目中，我们谈谈“好人”。我们有四个故事，其中一个是我陪Ben去尝试，看他是否有胆量通过自称好人来获取免费物品，尽管他担心尝试这个练习本身就意味着他根本不是个好人。因为朋友们，要明确什么是一个好人会做或不会做的事，有时确实是个难题。在今天我们的每个故事中，都有人在努力寻找那条界限，试图停在界限的一侧，在这个众所周知非常困难的世界里努力做个“好人”。

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:06.870
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《This American Life》。我是Ira Glass。请留下来继续收听。

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:19.300
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Takes One to Know One." So after 11 months of stalling, Ben finally headed out to get himself the good guy discount wearing a microphone hidden underneath his shirt. I went with him.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“物以类聚”。在拖延了11个月后，Ben终于戴着藏在衬衫下的麦克风，准备去争取“好人折扣”了。我和他一起去了。

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:27.040
<v Ben Calhoun>First place we go, a baby store. It's a big national chain. I've bought stuff at this particular store before.
<v Ben Calhoun>我们去的第一个地方是一家婴儿用品店。这是一家大型连锁店。我以前在这家店买过东西。

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:40.140
<v Ben Calhoun>And for our test, I chose one of these baby items that's mind blowingly expensive, so much so that it's hard to imagine anyone actually buying it. It's a blanket for a stroller. It cost $190.
<v Ben Calhoun>为了测试，我挑选了一件贵得离谱的婴儿用品，以至于很难想象有人真的会买它。那是一个婴儿车的毯子，售价190美元。

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:49.210
<v Ben Calhoun>My thought in doing this is that the markup is probably so insane that it'd make it easier to give a discount. Anyway, so I grab one and I get in line.
<v Ben Calhoun>我想这样做的话，利润空间可能高得离谱，或许更容易给出折扣。总之，我拿了一个，排队结账。

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.600
<v Ben Calhoun>Now I feel nervous.
<v Ben Calhoun>现在我感到很紧张。

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:58.960
<v Ira Glass>The whole pitch still makes me queasy. But I'm committed to selling it as best I can. There's one cashier working.
<v Ira Glass>整个推销话术仍然让我感到恶心。但我下定决心要尽我所能把它推销出去。柜台只有一个收银员。

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.440
<v Ben Calhoun>Hey, how you doing today?
<v Ben Calhoun>嘿，今天过得怎么样？

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:01.560
<v Cashier 1>I'm good, and you?
<v Cashier 1>很好，你呢？

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.700
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, good.
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，挺好。

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:07.880
<v Ben Calhoun>What's the total on that?
<v Ben Calhoun>那个总共多少钱？

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.720
<v Cashier 1>$206.85.
<v Cashier 1>206.85美元。

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:13.280
<v Ben Calhoun>Is there any way I could get a good guy discount on that?
<v Ben Calhoun>请问我能享受某种“好人折扣”吗？

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.780
<v Cashier 1>Good guy discount? I don't think we do those.
<v Cashier 1>“好人折扣”？我不觉得我们有这种东西。

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.390
<v Ben Calhoun>[LAUGHS]
<v Ben Calhoun>[笑]

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:28.230
<v Ira Glass>It's hard to hear. But when I ask for the discount, she cracks a smile and kind of laughs and says, "Good guy discount? We don't do those."
<v Ira Glass>听不太清。但当我要求折扣时，她露出了微笑，有点想笑，说道：“‘好人折扣’？我们不提供那个。”

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:34.700
<v Ben Calhoun>Are there any promotions coming up? Or is this going to go on sale any time soon or anything? No, nothing?
<v Ben Calhoun>最近有什么促销活动吗？或者这东西很快会打折吗？没有吗？

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:40.320
<v Ira Glass>I leave the thing on the counter. So first attempt, we're 0 for one.
<v Ira Glass>我把东西留在了柜台上。所以第一次尝试，我们失败了。

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:50.170
<v Ira Glass>Ira felt like I should have tried harder. And for the next one, I should keep the thing going longer, lean harder on the schtick. I'm a good guy, you're a good guy. What do you say?
<v Ira Glass>Ira觉得我应该再努力一点。下次，我应该把戏演得长一点，更坚持那套说辞。我是好人，你是好人。你觉得怎么样？

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:53.740
<v Ira Glass>I am not so sure. So next stop.
<v Ira Glass>我不确定。所以，下一站。

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.600
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, I guess I'll try the nine first. Do they run a little big?
<v Ben Calhoun>是的，我想我先试试九号。它们码数偏大吗？

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:06.150
<v Ira Glass>This item? Winter boots. We go to a big, big sporting goods store, though not a chain, just in case some strict corporate policy had been the problem at the baby store.
<v Ira Glass>这个商品是冬靴。我们去了另一家非常大的体育用品商店，不过不是连锁店，以防婴儿用品店的问题是源于严格的公司政策。

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.600
<v Cashier 2>The following customer can step down.
<v Cashier 2>下一位顾客可以过来。

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:20.570
<v Ben Calhoun>At the registers, I get called up. There's a woman, maybe late 20s. She's got a lot of makeup. She looks fed up with something. My thought is, I'll smile a little more, be a little more friendly.
<v Ben Calhoun>到了收银台，我被叫了过去。那是个女人，大概不到30岁。她化了浓妆，看起来对什么事很不耐烦。我的想法是，我会多笑一点，表现得更友善一些。

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:26.120
<v Ben Calhoun>And I know this is going to sound silly. Is there any way-- is there any kind of good guy discount I can get on those?
<v Ben Calhoun>我知道这听起来很傻。请问我能在这个上面享受任何类型的“好人折扣”吗？

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:29.400
<v Cashier 2>No. We don't offer-- it's just whatever the vendor sells it for.
<v Cashier 2>不行。我们不提供——价格就是供应商定的。

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.670
<v Ben Calhoun>So there's no promotions that I'm missing out on?
<v Ben Calhoun>所以没有什么我错过的促销活动吗？

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:33.470
<v Cashier 2>No.
<v Cashier 2>没有。

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:36.200
<v Ben Calhoun>0 for two. She sends me on my way.
<v Ben Calhoun>两次失败。她把我打发走了。

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:37.820
<v Cashier 2>There you go. Have a good one.
<v Cashier 2>给您。祝你过得愉快。

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:45.050
<v Ben Calhoun>Have a good one. It's like have a nice day with all the friendly burned off. So Ira and I head outside.
<v Ben Calhoun>你也是。这听起来就像是去掉了所有友好成分的“祝你愉快”。所以Ira和我走到外面。

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:50.030
<v Ira Glass>You didn't push it very far. You still just kept it to one sentence before you caved.
<v Ira Glass>你没怎么推销。你还没开始就妥协了，只说了一句话。

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:55.830
<v Ben Calhoun>No, but I came back with, so there's not any sales or promotions that I'm missing out on? And she was like--
<v Ben Calhoun>不，但我追问了：“所以没有什么我错过的促销活动吗？”然后她就——

00:08:55.830 --> 00:09:00.420
<v Ira Glass>But that's different than selling her the good guy discount.
<v Ira Glass>但这和推销“好人折扣”是两码事。

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:04.410
<v Ben Calhoun>You mean being like, come on, I'm a good guy.
<v Ben Calhoun>你是说像这样：“拜托，我是个好人。”

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:07.950
<v Ira Glass>Uh  huh. Yeah, you still haven't said that.
<v Ira Glass>呃，是的。你还没说过那句话呢。

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:13.350
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, that feels-- but you know what? I feel like I could tell that she was going to say no.
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，那感觉——但你知道吗？我觉得我看得出来她会说不。

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.740
<v Ira Glass>No. No, I couldn't tell.
<v Ira Glass>不，不。我看不出来。

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:15.680
<v Ben Calhoun>And at that point, all you're getting is humiliated.
<v Ben Calhoun>到了那个点，你只会感到羞辱。

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:22.820
<v Ira Glass>We go around and around, until we remember, oh right, we can just find out. Ira goes back in.
<v Ira Glass>我们反复讨论，直到我们想起：噢，对了，我们可以直接去问。Ira回到店里。

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:27.700
<v Ira Glass>My friend was just in here and he tried to get a discount from you? He asked you for a good guy discount?
<v Ira Glass>我朋友刚才来过这里，他想向你要个折扣？他问你要“好人折扣”？

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:28.680
<v Cashier 2>Yeah.
<v Cashier 2>是的。

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:29.670
<v Ira Glass>Like five minutes ago?
<v Ira Glass>就在五分钟前？

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:30.890
<v Cashier 2>Yes. Yes, I recall.
<v Cashier 2>是的，是的，我有印象。

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:34.870
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. If he had pushed harder, could he have gotten a discount?
<v Ira Glass>如果他更坚持一点，他能得到折扣吗？

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:35.580
<v Cashier 2>No.
<v Cashier 2>不能。

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:44.510
<v Ira Glass>If he had been more charming, do you get an employee discount that you could have swiped a card and given him? Do you have any kind of discount at all? Do you have any kind of power to give him?
<v Ira Glass>如果他更有魅力一点呢？你有没有员工折扣可以刷卡给他？你有没有任何折扣？你有任何权力给他折扣吗？

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.250
<v Cashier 2>No. It's just the store policy.
<v Cashier 2>没有。这是商店的政策。

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:50.620
<v Ira Glass>So if your sister came in here or something, could you give he discount?
<v Ira Glass>所以如果你的姐妹来这里，你能给她折扣吗？

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:51.570
<v Cashier 2>No.
<v Cashier 2>不能。

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:52.600
<v Ira Glass>But you get a discount.
<v Ira Glass>但你有折扣啊。

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:56.910
<v Cashier 2>Right, which I'm rung up by another fellow manager or associate.
<v Cashier 2>对，那是我由其他经理或同事结账时用的。

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:04.400
<v Ben Calhoun>See? All of this brings us to attempt number three, record store. Yeah, they do still exist.
<v Ben Calhoun>看到了吧？所有这些导致了第三次尝试，唱片店。是的，它们依然存在。

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:15.380
<v Ben Calhoun>I grab what should amount to $70 worth of records. I go to the counter. Behind it, there's a serious record store guy-- black hoodie, black wool hat, hair sticking out of the front.
<v Ben Calhoun>我拿了价值70美元的唱片。我走向柜台。后面站着一个严肃的唱片店伙计——黑色连帽衫，黑色羊毛帽，头发从前面冒出来。

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:16.290
<v Cashier 3>How you doing?
<v Cashier 3>你好吗？

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:19.300
<v Ben Calhoun>Hey, is there any way I could get a good guy discount on those?
<v Ben Calhoun>嘿，请问我能买这些唱片享受“好人折扣”吗？

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.240
<v Cashier 3>No. No.
<v Cashier 3>不能。不行。

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:21.920
<v Ben Calhoun>Not like a bulk?
<v Ben Calhoun>批发折扣也不行吗？

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.440
<v Cashier 3>No. Not for new stuff.
<v Cashier 3>不行。新货没有折扣。

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:23.840
<v Ben Calhoun>No?
<v Ben Calhoun>不给吗？

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.740
<v Cashier 3>No. 'Cause we barely make a profit on that stuff. Yeah, so I'm afraid not.
<v Cashier 3>不给。因为我们卖这些东西几乎没利润。所以，恐怕不行。

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:37.700
<v Ben Calhoun>I mean, so many noes in such a tiny, tiny answer. I counted them. Five nos in 8.7 seconds. It's like a Russian nesting doll of no.
<v Ben Calhoun>我是说，在这么简短的回答里，他说了这么多“不”。我数了数。8.7秒内说了五个“不”。简直像套娃一样的拒绝。

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:51.600
<v Ben Calhoun>And really, I would argue it's hard to say no that thoroughly, unless you actually want to say no. Unless you think you're being asked for something stupid by someone stupid. This is what I believed would happen.
<v Ben Calhoun>说真的，我想除非你真的想拒绝，否则很难把话说得这么绝。除非你觉得有人在问你蠢问题。这就是我预想会发生的事情。

00:10:51.600 --> 00:11:01.840
<v Ben Calhoun>And this one, it makes me 0 for three. And I gotta say, it felt bad. And it raises the obvious question, what does Sonari have that I don't?
<v Ben Calhoun>而这一次，我三次全败。我不得不说，感觉很差。这引发了一个明显的问题：Sonari有什么是我没有的？

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:16.460
<v Ben Calhoun>As we headed back to the office, Ira said, maybe he's just more charming and better looking. So I called Sonari, and I asked him something that I probably should have asked him before, which is exactly how often does this work for him?
<v Ben Calhoun>当我们回到办公室时，Ira说，也许他只是更有魅力、长得更好看。所以我给Sonari打了电话，问了他一个我本来应该早点问的问题，那就是，这招对他来说到底有多频繁地成功？

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:20.530
<v Sonari Glinton>I would say 15% to 20% of the time.
<v Sonari Glinton>我想大概15%到20%的时间吧。

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:24.370
<v Ben Calhoun>20%? That's actually a lot of noes.
<v Ben Calhoun>20%？那其实会有很多次被拒绝。

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:31.940
<v Ben Calhoun>He only gets one out of five. I tried three times. So I needed two more tries. Fine.
<v Ben Calhoun>他每五次才能成功一次。我试了三次。所以我还需要两次尝试。好吧。

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:32.340
<v Ben Calhoun>Hey.
<v Ben Calhoun>嘿。

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:33.130
<v Cashier 4>Hey.
<v Cashier 4>嘿。

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:38.490
<v Ben Calhoun>So here we are, number four. The item is a clock. And here we go.
<v Ben Calhoun>我们到了，这是第四次。商品是一个时钟。我们要开始了。

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:41.970
<v Ben Calhoun>Is there any way that you guys would give a good guy discount on that?
<v Ben Calhoun>请问你们能在这个上面给个“好人折扣”吗？

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:43.250
<v Cashier 4>No, I'm sorry.
<v Cashier 4>不行，很抱歉。

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:45.690
<v Ben Calhoun>No, not at all?
<v Ben Calhoun>完全不行吗？

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:59.050
<v Ira Glass>0 for four. I head to a small cookware shop. And if Sonari's stats are one in five, this is the big game. Do or die time. If the good guy discount is real for me, then this is it.
<v Ira Glass>四次全败。我去了家小型厨具店。如果Sonari的成功率是五分之一，那这次就是决战时刻。成败在此一举。如果“好人折扣”对我来说是真的，那现在就是了。

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:02.270
<v Ben Calhoun>Can I ask you, this All-Clad--
<v Ben Calhoun>能问一下吗，这个All-Clad锅——

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:03.060
<v Cashier 4>Mm hmm?
<v Cashier 4>嗯？

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:08.600
<v Ben Calhoun>Pot up here, would you guys ever give a good guy discount on this?
<v Ben Calhoun>这个锅，你们会给“好人折扣”吗？

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:09.070
<v Cashier 4>On All-Clad?
<v Cashier 4>All-Clad的？

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:09.650
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah.
<v Ben Calhoun>对。

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:13.400
<v Cashier 4>You know, it's hard. I could probably do about 5%.
<v Cashier 4>你知道，这很难。我或许可以给5%的折扣。

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:24.320
<v Ben Calhoun>Like the sword from the stone, my friend. Behold, the good guy discount. Apparently, it can work for anybody. It'll work for you.
<v Ben Calhoun>就像石中剑一样，我的朋友。瞧，这就是“好人折扣”。显然，它对谁都管用。它对你也管用。

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:34.730
<v Ben Calhoun>But can we just stop for a second? Just stop. And I know that this is going to sound super earnest, and I'm real sorry about that. But I believe it, so I gotta say it.
<v Ben Calhoun>但是我们能先停一下吗？真的。我知道这听起来非常真诚，我很抱歉。但我相信这一点，所以我必须说出来。

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:47.350
<v Ben Calhoun>I don't think that you should try it. I'm not gonna try it again. Sorry, Sonari. I just don't think that we should go throwing around the term good guy like it's some kind of coupon. I think that good guy means something.
<v Ben Calhoun>我不认为你应该尝试。我不会再试了。抱歉，Sonari。我只是觉得我们不应该到处乱用“好人”这个词，把它当成某种优惠券。我认为“好人”是有含义的。

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:58.170
<v Ben Calhoun>Just the other day, I was with my son and my dad, and my kid is eating cake. He's two. He loves cakes. And he's got one bite left. And I say, can I have a bite?
<v Ben Calhoun>就在几天前，我和儿子还有我父亲在一起，我孩子在吃蛋糕。他两岁，很爱吃。他只剩最后一口了。我说：“我能吃一口吗？”

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:19.890
<v Ben Calhoun>And he looks up at me and he holds out his hand, offers me his last bite. And my dad says, what a good guy. When I was a kid, he used to say that to me too. I like the way the world looks during those moments. I think I'd rather have whatever that is than a discount.
<v Ben Calhoun>他抬头看着我，伸出手，把最后一口递给了我。我父亲说：“真是个好人。”我小时候，他也会这样对我说。我喜欢在那些时刻看到的这个世界。我想我宁愿要那种感觉，而不是什么折扣。

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:52.060
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun.
<v Ira Glass>Ben Calhoun。

00:13:52.060 --> 00:14:05.420
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "The Heels on the Bus." So a test of whether or not you are a good guy, it can happen anywhere, as Mike Birbiglia well knows. He's a comedian and told this story onstage in New York.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“公交车上的变态”。所以，测试你是否是好人，这随时随地都有可能发生，正如Mike Birbiglia所知。他是一位喜剧演员，曾在纽约的舞台上讲述了这个故事。

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:20.510
<v Mike Birbiglia>Last week I was meeting my wife in the city for dinner. And I went down to take the subway. And one of the unfortunate things about living in New York with the subway-- and you guys probably know this-- is sometimes on the weekends the subway is just off.
<v Mike Birbiglia>上周，我约了妻子在城里吃晚饭。我下楼去乘地铁。在纽约生活，乘坐地铁有个不幸的事情——你们可能知道——有时周末地铁会暂停服务。

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:21.550
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:37.440
<v Mike Birbiglia>And there'll just be a sign. There's just like, there isn't the subway. You know, and it'll say, you could take the bus. You know, it's written in pencil and there's an ellipsis, you know.
<v Mike Birbiglia>那里只会贴个告示。就像是说，没有地铁了。你知道，是用铅笔写的，后面还有个省略号。

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:54.860
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so last weekend that happened. And so I walked up to the bus and I said to the bus driver, hey, the subway's off. But it said that I could take the bus to the subway. Do you know where I can get off? And the bus driver said to me-- and I quote-- he goes, I don't know anything about the subway.
<v Mike Birbiglia>上周末就发生了这种情况。我走到公交车站，对公交司机说：“嘿，地铁停运了。但上面写着我可以乘公交车去地铁站。你知道我该在哪里下车吗？”公交司机对我说——引用他的原话——他说：“我不知道关于地铁的任何事。”

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:58.110
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:07.350
<v Mike Birbiglia>I was like, you don't know anything about the subway? You're wearing the same outfit as the man running the subway.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说：“你不知道地铁的事？你穿着和地铁司机一样的制服。”

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:23.030
<v Mike Birbiglia>If someone said to me, you see that guy walking down the street with the khaki shorts and the Kings of Leon t-shirt? You know anything about that guy? I'd be like, yeah, I'll tell you a few things about that guy. I know that he could use somebody.
<v Mike Birbiglia>如果有人对我说：“你看到那个穿着卡其短裤和Kings of Leon乐队T恤走在街上的人了吗？你知道关于那家伙的事吗？”我会说：“是啊，我能告诉你一些关于他的事。我知道他需要有人帮忙。”

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:28.860
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:45.310
<v Mike Birbiglia>There was only one other person on the bus. And it was this guy. And he looked up at me and he goes, I'm taking the subway. You can follow me.
<v Mike Birbiglia>公交车上只有另一个人。就是那个家伙。他抬头看我，说道：“我也去乘地铁。你可以跟着我。”

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:52.360
<v Mike Birbiglia>So now I'm in action film. I'm like, got it. I sit down, and I'm following this guy.
<v Mike Birbiglia>于是现在我像是置身于动作片里了。我想：好，明白了。我坐下，跟着这个家伙。

00:15:52.360 --> 00:16:10.960
<v Mike Birbiglia>And then we go a few stops and this lady walks on to the bus. And she was a pretty lady. And she wasn't just bus pretty. She was pretty for the world.
<v Mike Birbiglia>车开了几站，一位女士上了公交车。她很漂亮。那种美不仅限于公交车的美，她是全世界公认的美。

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:31.550
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I did this thing that I sometimes do when I see a pretty lady, which is I looked at her long enough to realize that she was pretty, and then I looked at the floor. And then I never looked up again. That's my move.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我做了一件我看到漂亮女士时有时会做的事，那就是：我看着她直到意识到她很漂亮，然后我就盯着地板看。之后我再也没抬过头。这就是我的套路。

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:46.760
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so I'm looking at the floor. And then I'm looking up at this guy I'm about to follow, 'cause he's like my ride. But then he was staring at the lady.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以我盯着地板。然后又看着我要跟随的那个家伙，因为他带我走。但接着，他一直盯着那位女士看。

00:16:46.760 --> 00:17:14.410
<v Mike Birbiglia>And you know, I know we're all guilty of staring at some point or another. I think we've all done it. But I think that there's an etiquette to staring. I think that you can use your peripheral position if you want to look at somebody. You can stare at someone like you're inside of a spooky painting in Scooby-Doo where your face doesn't move, but then your eyes pan side to side like you're living in the walls of a mansion.
<v Mike Birbiglia>你知道，我知道我们都曾盯着某人看过。我想我们都做过。但我认为盯着人看是有礼仪的。如果你想看某人，你可以利用余光。你可以盯着某人看，就像《史酷比》里那种阴森的画一样，脸不动，但眼睛来回扫描，就像住在豪宅的墙里。

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:21.890
<v Mike Birbiglia>But this guy was not doing the spooky painting. He was just full on staring at this lady, like a gargoyle.
<v Mike Birbiglia>但那家伙做的不是阴森画。他就像个石像鬼一样盯着那女士。

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:43.300
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I was so mad. I was just like, we all want to stare at the lady. But we don't, because we are decent people. We have decided as a group that we are not going to stare at the pretty lady.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我太生气了。我心想：我们都想盯着那女士看。但我们没有，因为我们是体面人。我们作为一个群体已经决定了，我们不会盯着漂亮女士看。

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:56.100
<v Mike Birbiglia>But that's the difference between decent people and creepy people. Creepy people do the things that decent people want to do but have decided are probably not a great idea.
<v Mike Birbiglia>但这就是体面人和变态的区别。变态做了体面人想做但决定最好别做的事情。

00:17:56.100 --> 00:18:11.940
<v Mike Birbiglia>Decent people are like, I would like to stare at that pretty lady, but it will make her feel uncomfortable. Creepy people are like, look. I'm going for it.
<v Mike Birbiglia>体面人会想：“我很想盯着那位美女看，但那会让她感到不安。”变态会想：“看吧，我就要看了。”

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:27.610
<v Mike Birbiglia>We go a few more stops and the creepy guy stands up, looks at me, and says, let's go. I was like, oh great. Now she thinks I'm with you.
<v Mike Birbiglia>又过了几站，那个变态站起来，看着我说：“走吧。”我想：噢，太好了。现在她以为我和你是一伙的了。

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:45.560
<v Mike Birbiglia>This pretty lady and I have built up a rapport where we have an understanding that she is pretty and I am decent. And when I am in her presence, I stare at the floor. And now she thinks I am with you, a creepy man?
<v Mike Birbiglia>这位美女和我建立了一种默契，她知道她很美，我也很体面。当我在她面前时，我盯着地板看。而现在她认为我和你，一个变态，是一伙的？

00:18:45.560 --> 00:19:20.410
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so as I walk by this woman, I try to convey all of this with my eyes. Like, I am not with this guy. And that look takes so long to convey that the look itself becomes creepy. And I know it, and so I say, sorry, which were the first words uttered in a conversation we were not having.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以当我从那个女人身边走过时，我试着用眼睛表达所有这些。像是在说：“我不认识这人。”这种眼神表达太久了，以至于眼神本身变得变态起来。我意识到了，所以我说了声“抱歉”，这成了我们还没开始的对话中的第一句词。

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:50.920
<v Mike Birbiglia>As this woman understands her bus ride, she walked onto a bus, a creepy man stared at her for 10 minutes and then left the bus. And then another man, who she did not know was on the bus, approached her and apologized, which means what he did is worse.
<v Mike Birbiglia>这位女士对公交行程的理解是：她上了一辆车，一个变态盯着她看了10分钟，然后离开了。接着另一个她本来不知道在车上的男人靠近她并道歉，这意味着他做的事情更糟糕。

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:58.250
<v Mike Birbiglia>[APPLAUSE]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[掌声]

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:15.140
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I'm so frustrated about the unfairness of the situation that when I get to the dinner with my wife, I'm explaining this whole thing in detail. And I'm like, it's not fair because I am not creepy. I am decent.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我对这种情况的不公感到沮丧，所以当我赶到餐厅和妻子共进晚餐时，我详细地解释了这一切。我说：“这不公平，因为我不是变态。我是体面人。”

00:20:15.140 --> 00:20:30.730
<v Mike Birbiglia>And my wife says, wait. Is the moral of the story that you want credit for just not being creepy? And I was like, exactly.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我妻子说：“等等。故事的寓意是你想因为‘没有变成变态’而获得奖励吗？”我说：“正是如此。”

00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:41.710
<v Mike Birbiglia>Because I think that decent people are undervalued. I think that decent people should be treated like firemen. We should get a day and a calendar.
<v Mike Birbiglia>因为我认为体面人被低估了。我认为体面人应该像消防员一样被对待。我们应该有自己的节日。

00:20:41.710 --> 00:21:16.080
<v Mike Birbiglia>One month there'd just be a guy carrying in porch furniture for the winter. One month there'd just be a guy grilling a veggie burger with an apron that says "Meatless in Seattle." My month, it'd just be me checking out at a pharmacy with tampons and a yogurt.
<v Mike Birbiglia>某个月会有一群人帮邻居把门廊家具搬进屋避冬。某个月会有一群人戴着写着“西雅图无肉”的围裙烤素汉堡。我的月份里，我也许就在药店结账，买了卫生棉和酸奶。

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:22.410
<v Mike Birbiglia>And my wife says, but you do get credit. I married you.
<v Mike Birbiglia>妻子说：“但你已经得到奖励了。我嫁给了你。”

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:24.580
<v Audience>Aww.
<v Audience>噢。

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:27.000
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I said, I want more than that.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说：“我想得到的比这更多。”

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:27.910
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:29.750
<v Mike Birbiglia>[APPLAUSE]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[掌声]

