<v Don Shirley>He thought of it more of a mission to actually-- his task to bring this body back.
<v Don Shirley>他认为这更像是一项任务——他的职责是把这具遗体带回来。

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:12.630
<v Julia Dewitt>So when Dave came up, he told Don he was going to come back and get Deon's body.
<v Julia Dewitt>所以当Dave浮出水面时，他告诉Don他打算回来把Deon的遗体带走。

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:21.210
<v Don Shirley>He phoned up Deon's parents and said, I'm going to retrieve your son's body.
<v Don Shirley>他打电话给Deon的父母说：“我要去带回你们儿子的遗体。”

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:28.050
<v Julia Dewitt>The thing is that a dive like this is a major operation. It took them months to plan.
<v Julia Dewitt>问题是，这样的潜水是一项重大行动。他们花了几个月来计划。

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:40.970
<v Don Shirley>You're at 900 foot under the water. There's a lot of risk involved in that. Every 33 foot that you go down effectively doubles the risk. When you're down at those depths, anything that goes wrong is an issue.
<v Don Shirley>你在水下900英尺。这涉及巨大的风险。你每下潜33英尺，风险基本上就翻倍。当你在这种深度时，任何出错的地方都是问题。

00:27:40.970 --> 00:27:57.010
<v Julia Dewitt>Combining extreme depths with the hard work that Dave had to do to get Deon's body into a body bag came with a lot of risk. At depth, too much nitrogen is kind of like a narcotic. Basically it feels suddenly like you drank five martinis in a row.
<v Julia Dewitt>将极端深度与Dave为了把遗体装入尸袋所需的辛苦工作结合起来，伴随着很大的风险。在深处，过量的氮就像一种麻醉剂。基本上你会突然觉得像一口气喝了五杯马提尼。

00:27:57.010 --> 00:28:08.900
<v Julia Dewitt>Too much helium can give you twitching fits. If you breathe too heavily, like Dave might have to while he's moving around Deon's body-- you pass out. And then of course, there's the bends.
<v Julia Dewitt>过量的氦会导致痉挛。如果你呼吸过重，比如Dave在移动遗体时可能必须要做的那样——你会昏迷。然后当然，还有减压病。

00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:19.690
<v Don Shirley>If you come up too quickly, it's like opening a Coke bottle once you've shook it up and it fizzes. And then you would have problems with bubbles in your blood.
<v Don Shirley>如果你浮起得太快，就像摇晃可乐瓶后打开瓶盖一样，会滋滋冒气。然后你的血液里会有气泡引发问题。

00:28:19.690 --> 00:28:33.300
<v Julia Dewitt>To prevent the bends, Dave would take several hours to come up to the surface. So they recruited a team of support divers that would go into the water at intervals to check on Dave at various depths while he came up.
<v Julia Dewitt>为了防止减压病，Dave需要几个小时才能浮出水面。所以他们招募了一组支持潜水员，在不同的深度分阶段下水，在Dave上升的过程中检查他的状态。

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:41.290
<v Don Shirley>And the rule was no one will go deeper than the depth where we actually plan for them to be.
<v Don Shirley>规则是：没人会比我们计划让他们到达的深度更深。

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:44.250
<v Julia Dewitt>Don would go the deepest.
<v Julia Dewitt>Don会去最深的地方。

00:28:44.250 --> 00:29:00.790
<v Don Shirley>But as far as Dave and I were concerned, basically what we said is if Dave has a problem, he would signal me. And in caving, when you flash a light, you wave a light around, that's a distress signal.
<v Don Shirley>但就Dave和我而言，我们基本上约定，如果Dave有问题，他会向我发出信号。在洞潜中，当你闪光、挥动灯光时，那就是求救信号。

00:29:00.790 --> 00:29:11.960
<v Don Shirley>We got to the day that we were actually planning to do the main dive. And we went down early in the morning. It was still dark. The sun was not quite up yet.
