<v Don Shirley>If you see the videos of me coming up, you'd think this guy's half dead. You know, it had been just over 12 hours in the water. I couldn't actually really do much for myself other than breathe. My head was like a marshmallow.
<v Don Shirley>如果你看我浮出水面的视频，你会觉得这人半死不活。你知道，在水下待了刚超过12个小时。除了呼吸，我实际上做不了多少事。我的头就像棉花糖一样。

00:34:20.140 --> 00:34:42.870
<v Julia Dewitt>Don was put into a decompression chamber and then taken in the morning for more treatment at a hospital in Johannesburg. Dave did not come up that night. Dave was dead. And then a week later, Don got some news.
<v Julia Dewitt>Don被放进了减压舱，第二天被带到约翰内斯堡的一家医院进行更多治疗。Dave那天晚上没有浮上来。Dave死了。一周后，Don得到了一些消息。

00:34:42.870 --> 00:34:53.670
<v Don Shirley>I actually was told that Dave's body had come up. During that week, there'd been lots of speculation as to what had happened.
<v Don Shirley>我其实被告知Dave的遗体浮上来了。在那周里，对于发生了什么有很多推测。

00:34:53.670 --> 00:35:06.030
<v Don Shirley>Dave was carrying a camera on his head. In retrieving the camera, then we could piece together really what happened.
<v Don Shirley>Dave头上带着摄像头。找回摄像头后，我们就能还原到底发生了什么。

00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:12.020
<v Julia Dewitt>As the camera rolls, you see Dave trying to get Deon's body into a body bag.
<v Julia Dewitt>随着画面滚动，你看到Dave试图把Deon的遗体放进尸袋。

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:24.080
<v Don Shirley>One of his lights had actually got tangled up in the line and smashed. So now, bearing in mind you're in the pitch black, his main light had actually been broken. So he couldn't really see so well.
<v Don Shirley>他的一盏灯缠在了绳子上并撞碎了。请记住你身处漆黑一片中，他的主灯坏了。所以他看不清。

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:31.990
<v Julia Dewitt>Then Dave also gets tangled. He pulls away, but now he's tied to Deon's body.
<v Julia Dewitt>然后Dave也被缠住了。他挣脱了，但现在他被绑在Deon的遗体上。

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:46.990
<v Don Shirley>And you could see that he was working trying to get himself out of this line. He was cutting with the scissors. But his scissors weren't even getting anywhere near the line.
<v Don Shirley>你可以看到他正在努力把自己从绳子里弄出来。他在用剪刀剪。但剪刀甚至根本没剪到绳子上。

00:35:46.990 --> 00:35:59.960
<v Julia Dewitt>Listening to the video, you can hear Dave's breathing start to get shallower and shallower as he starts working harder and harder.
<v Julia Dewitt>听着视频，你可以听到Dave的呼吸随着他越来越用力，开始变得越来越浅。

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:28.960
<v Don Shirley>Dave passed out from too much carbon dioxide. You know, he worked right up, right up to the very last breath that he ever took trying to get out. And then the camera just carries on recording until the batteries run out.
<v Don Shirley>Dave因为过多的二氧化碳昏迷了。你知道，他一直工作到他吸入的最后一口气，试图脱困。然后摄像头继续录制，直到电池耗尽。

00:36:28.960 --> 00:36:43.700
<v Don Shirley>There's barely a day goes by where I don't think of Dave. And really, there are a lot of times I turn round-- I do a fantastic dive and I just want to say, did you see that?
<v Don Shirley>几乎没有一天我不想到Dave。而且有很多时候我会转身——我完成了一次精彩的潜水，我只想说：“你看到那个了吗？”

00:36:43.700 --> 00:36:52.700
<v Don Shirley>And Dave very much did die nobly doing what he did. He did everything that he should do. And he died, as they'd say in the military, with his boots on.
<v Don Shirley>Dave确实高尚地死在了他的岗位上。他做了他该做的一切。他就像军队里所说的那样，死在了阵地上。

00:36:52.700 --> 00:37:10.560
<v Don Shirley>When Dave's body had come up, hanging underneath Dave's body, cocooned in the line that Dave originally had laid, was Deon Dreyer's body. So Dave actually achieved what he wanted to do.
<v Don Shirley>当Dave的遗体浮上来时，悬在Dave遗体下方的，茧在Dave最初铺设的绳子里的，正是Deon Dreyer的遗体。所以Dave确实达成了他想做的事。

00:37:10.560 --> 00:38:31.980
<v Ira Glass>This story came from Julia DeWitt. It was first broadcast on the public radio show Snap Judgment . If you want to hear more stories like that one, Snap Judgment is on many public radio stations. And it's also a podcast, which you can find at snapjudgment.org or the iTunes store or wherever it is that you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>这个故事来自Julia DeWitt。它最初是在公共广播节目《Snap Judgment》上播出的。如果你想听更多那样的故事，《Snap Judgment》在许多公共广播电台都有播出。它也是个播客，你可以在snapjudgment.org或者iTunes商店，或者任何你获取播客的地方找到它。

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:41.280
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, "The Test." So all this hour, we've been hearing about guys trying to be good guys and being tested and trying to do the right thing.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“测试”。在这一小时里，我们一直在听关于人们试图成为好人、被考验并试图做正确之事的各种故事。

00:38:41.280 --> 00:39:00.530
<v Ira Glass>But of course, sometimes that is really hard. Sometimes you're not up to it. You look around at your life and you realize things are not exactly right, and it's not clear how to fix them and how to be a good guy. And so you just take a step forward on whatever path appears right then in front of you, whatever that path is.
<v Ira Glass>当然，有时这真的很难。有时你应付不来。你环顾自己的生活，意识到事情并不完全正确，也不清楚该如何解决，该如何做一个好人。所以你只是顺着眼前出现的任何路径向前迈了一步，无论那条路是什么。

00:39:00.530 --> 00:39:11.090
<v Scott Carrier>I was hired to interview men and women in the state of Utah who receive Medicaid support for treatment of mental illnesses generally diagnosed as schizophrenia.
<v Scott Carrier>我受聘去采访犹他州接受医疗补助以治疗通常被诊断为精神分裂症的心理疾病的男女。

00:39:11.090 --> 00:39:26.470
<v Ira Glass>That's Scott Carrier, who took that job after working on and off as a radio reporter out of Salt Lake City. He was hired for the interviewing job because the director of this research project heard some of Scott's radio stories and thought he was a good interviewer, somebody who knew how to listen.
<v Ira Glass>那是Scott Carrier，他在盐湖城断断续续做广播记者后接受了那份工作。他受聘做这项采访工作，是因为这个研究项目的负责人听过他的一些广播报道，认为他是个很好的采访者，懂得如何倾听。

00:39:26.470 --> 00:39:36.910
<v Ira Glass>The researchers taught Scott how to administer this test, which measured mental health. It was 100 questions, each of which was scored on a scale of one to seven.
<v Ira Glass>研究人员教Scott如何执行这项测量心理健康的测试。共有100个问题，每个问题都按1到7的等级评分。

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:44.390
<v Ira Glass>Took an hour to give the test. Scott was paid $30 for each test he gave. The people he gave the test to got $5.
<v Ira Glass>测试需要一小时。Scott每做一个测试获得30美元酬劳。他采访的人每人获得5美元。

00:39:44.390 --> 00:40:07.930
<v Scott Carrier>I had little understanding of schizophrenia before I began, and I have little more understanding now. I took the job because I had no other. I took the job because I'd just quit my steady job, my professional job, after realizing that what I wanted more than anything was to put my boss on the floor and stand on his throat and watch him gag.
<v Scott Carrier>开始之前，我对精神分裂症所知甚少，现在依然如此。我接这份工作是因为没有别的。我接这份工作是因为我刚辞去了稳定的工作，专业的工作，在意识到我最想做的事是把老板按在地板上，踩在他的喉咙上看着他窒息之后。

00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:25.720
<v Scott Carrier>Then my wife moved out, took the kids and everything. She said, I've thought about it, and I really think it's the best thing for me at this time in my life.
<v Scott Carrier>然后妻子搬走了，带走了孩子和一切。她说：“我想过了，我真的认为这对我现阶段的生活来说是最好的选择。”

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:38.640
<v Scott Carrier>And so I took the job interviewing schizophrenics because it was offered to me and because it was all there seemed to be. And it seemed somehow predestined, a karmic response that could not be avoided.
<v Scott Carrier>所以我接受了这份采访精神分裂症患者的工作，因为它摆在面前，而且看起来只有这一份可做。这似乎是命中注定的，是一种无法回避的业力回应。

00:40:38.640 --> 00:41:12.330
<v Scott Carrier>It would only be temporary, something to get through the summer. And I was told that they needed someone willing to drive around the state through the small towns, searching out individuals who are often transient and prone to hiding. I like to drive. I like to travel. And I like the idea of pursuit. So I took the job, and did the job, and my life will never be the same.
<v Scott Carrier>这只是暂时的，只是为了熬过夏天。有人告诉我，他们需要一个愿意开车环绕该州穿过小城镇的人，寻找那些通常行踪不定且倾向于躲藏的个人。我喜欢开车。我喜欢旅行。我喜欢追求的想法。所以我接了这份工作，做了这份工作，我的生活再也回不去了。

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:29.140
<v Scott Carrier>The patient is 21 years old and has lived with his parents since his discharge from the Army. He has no friends, no recreational activities, and no social life. He spends his time writing and reading, but these activities do not give him any pleasure.
<v Scott Carrier>这位患者21岁，自从从军队退伍后就一直和父母住在一起。他没有朋友，没有娱乐活动，没有社交生活。他把时间花在写作和阅读上，但这些活动并没有给他带来任何快乐。

00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:53.720
<v Scott Carrier>He has lost weight, has general anxiety and loss of libido, and occasional feelings of unreality. He is worried about his unpredictable behavior, for example, getting down on all fours and chewing the grass because he was thinking what it would be like to be a cow.
<v Scott Carrier>他体重下降，有普遍的焦虑感和性欲减退，偶尔会有不真实感。他担心自己不可预测的行为，例如因为思考像牛一样是什么感觉而四肢着地吃草。

00:41:53.720 --> 00:42:07.400
<v Scott Carrier>The patient is 25 years old and believes that she is the devil and therefore responsible for all the evil in the world. She's not been out of her house for seven days, and only comes down from her room for meals.
<v Scott Carrier>这位患者25岁，相信自己是魔鬼，因此要为世界上所有的邪恶负责。她已经七天没出过家门了，只有用餐时才会下楼。

00:42:07.400 --> 00:42:29.540
<v Scott Carrier>A few days ago, her mother walked into her room and found her crying. She asked her mother what was the most painful punishment that one human being could inflict upon another. The mother tried to get the reason for this question and her daughter mumbled something about the devil having to be punished for the benefit of humanity, something about having to die for his sins.
<v Scott Carrier>几天前，她母亲走进房间发现她在哭。她问母亲人类能给予另一个人最痛苦的惩罚是什么。母亲试着问她原因，她女儿嘟囔着什么关于“魔鬼必须为了人类的利益受到惩罚”，以及什么“为了他的罪孽必须去死”。

00:42:29.540 --> 00:42:49.340
<v Scott Carrier>When the mother asked her if she still thought she was the devil, she answered, let's not get into that again. It only upsets you and you don't believe me anyway, even when the evidence is all around you, plain for you to see.
<v Scott Carrier>当母亲问她是否仍然认为自己是魔鬼时，她回答：“我们别再谈这个了。这只会让你难过，而且你反正也不相信我，即使证据就在周围，显而易见。”

00:42:49.340 --> 00:43:16.440
<v Scott Carrier>The people I interview are all so sad, so lonely with such thin souls, like ghosts and demons have invaded their hearts and are sucking their souls dry. A person's soul should be like an ocean. But a schizophrenic's soul is like a pool of rain in a parking lot. They suffer and they are completely alone in their suffering, and there's nothing I can do, nothing anyone can do to bring them back.
<v Scott Carrier>我采访的人都如此悲伤、如此孤独，灵魂如此薄弱，就像幽灵和魔鬼入侵了他们的心，正在把他们的灵魂吸干。一个人的灵魂应该像大海。但精神分裂症患者的灵魂就像停车场里的雨水坑。他们受苦，在痛苦中完全孤独，我无能为力，没人能把他们拉回来。

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:22.970
<v Scott Carrier>I come home at night and cry. I sob like a three-year-old.
<v Scott Carrier>我晚上回到家就会哭。像个三岁小孩一样啜泣。

00:43:22.970 --> 00:43:30.430
<v Scott Carrier>Today, halfway through an interview with a man in Tooele, he says, I have a crystal in my pouch. Do you want to see it?
<v Scott Carrier>今天，在Tooele采访一个男人到一半时，他说：“我的小袋子里有个水晶。你想看看吗？”

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:45.600
<v Scott Carrier>I say OK and he takes it out, a normal crystal the size of a large paper clip. And he says, I can look through this, and it will tell me whether you're a good person or a bad person. What do you want me to do? Do you want me to look through it or not?
<v Scott Carrier>我说OK，他把它拿了出来，是一个正常的大号回形针大小的水晶。他说：“我可以透过它看，它会告诉我你是好人还是坏人。你想让我怎么做？你想让我透过它看还是不看？”

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:59.000
<v Scott Carrier>My first thought is to say, do you want to go on with the interview? Maybe when we're done, you can look through the crystal. But then I realize that he's really asking me to take his test, just like I'm asking him to take mine.
<v Scott Carrier>我首先想到的是说：“你想继续采访吗？也许等我们做完了，你可以透过水晶看看。”但随后我意识到他是在让我参加他的测试，就像我在让他参加我的测试一样。

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:07.160
<v Scott Carrier>I come into his house. I ask him very personal questions, and I expect him to answer honestly. And why should he?
<v Scott Carrier>我走进他的屋子。我问他非常私人的问题，我期望他诚实回答。可为什么他要诚实呢？

00:44:07.160 --> 00:44:18.970
<v Scott Carrier>So I say, OK, go ahead. And he puts the crystal up to his eye, turns it clockwise and counterclockwise, back and forth, squinting, looking me up and down.
<v Scott Carrier>所以我说：“OK，请吧。”他把水晶放到眼前，顺时针和逆时针转动，来回反复，眯起眼睛，上下打量着我。

00:44:18.970 --> 00:44:25.580
<v Scott Carrier>And he says, I can't tell for sure. I'm going to have to read your mind. Here, take my hand.
<v Scott Carrier>他说：“我不能确定。我得读读你的思想。来，握住我的手。”

00:44:25.580 --> 00:44:37.110
<v Scott Carrier>He holds out his right hand with the crystal resting in the palm. I take his hand, and he puts his left hand over mine and squeezes it tight and shakes it and goes into a small spasm.
<v Scott Carrier>他伸出右手，水晶躺在掌心。我握住他的手，他用左手盖住我的手，紧紧捏住，摇晃着，然后陷入了轻微的痉挛。

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:46.090
<v Scott Carrier>Then he lets go and sort of sits back like he's exhausted. He asks me if I felt anything. And I say, maybe a little.
<v Scott Carrier>然后他放开了，像精疲力竭一样坐了回去。他问我有没有感觉到什么。我说：“也许有一点。”

00:44:46.090 --> 00:44:58.160
<v Scott Carrier>And he says, I sent you a message. I put it in your mind. I told you what is wrong with me.
<v Scott Carrier>他说：“我发信息给你了。我把它传进了你的思想。我告诉你我有什么问题了。”

00:44:58.160 --> 00:45:19.490
<v Scott Carrier>I'm not supposed to figure out what's wrong with these people. I'm just supposed to ask the questions and score the answers from one to seven. This is partly because I'm not a doctor, and might get something going that I wouldn't know how to contain. But it's mainly because my supervisors want clean data. They want all the people asking the questions to be doing it in the same way.
<v Scott Carrier>我不应该去搞清楚这些人有什么问题。我只是应该问问题，给答案打1到7分。这部分是因为我不是医生，可能会引发我无法控制的事情。但主要是因为我的主管们需要干净的数据。他们想要所有问问题的人都以同样的方式去做。

00:45:19.490 --> 00:45:24.770
<v Scott Carrier>I'm not supposed to get emotional. I'm not supposed to let the patient get emotional.
<v Scott Carrier>我不应该情绪化。我不应该让患者情绪化。

00:45:24.770 --> 00:45:44.210
<v Scott Carrier>The therapy part of the county mental health system is in another department. I wouldn't even know what number to call. And I've been told more than once not to worry about it. I should never have let him take the crystal out of his pouch.
<v Scott Carrier>县心理健康系统的治疗部分在另一个部门。我甚至不知道该打什么号码。我被不止一次告知不要担心。我根本不该让他把水晶从袋子里拿出来。

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:55.050
<v Scott Carrier>I drove around all day trying to find a Navajo man. He lives very close to the Four Corners, the cross where Utah, Colorado, New Mexico, and Arizona meet.
<v Scott Carrier>我开车绕了一整天试图找一个纳瓦霍人。他住在离四角地（Four Corners）很近的地方，即犹他州、科罗拉多州、新墨西哥州和亚利桑那州的交界处。

00:45:55.050 --> 00:46:07.180
<v Scott Carrier>It's all dirt roads, a house every five miles or so. No addresses, no phones. I stop at every house and knock on the door. But either nobody's home or nobody will answer.
<v Scott Carrier>全是土路，大概每五英里有一座房子。没有地址，没有电话。我在每栋房子前停下敲门。但要么没人，要么没人回答。

00:46:07.180 --> 00:46:26.320
<v Scott Carrier>I flag down every car that passes and ask directions, and the people offer complicated directions that I follow as best as possible, sometimes driving for 20 or 30 miles. But it's always the wrong place or nobody's home or there just isn't a house there at all.
<v Scott Carrier>我拦住每辆路过的车问路，人们给出的复杂的路线，我尽可能遵循，有时开了20或30英里。但总是在错的地方，或者没人，或者那里根本没有房子。

00:46:26.320 --> 00:46:37.930
<v Scott Carrier>Driving around, I think about how I have some of the same problems as the people I interview. I'm angry, depressed, prone to paranoid delusions, and I worry a lot.
<v Scott Carrier>开车兜圈时，我想着我怎么有和我采访的人一样的问题。我很愤怒，沮丧，容易出现偏执妄想，而且我很担心。

00:46:37.930 --> 00:46:57.030
<v Scott Carrier>Up to now, I thought these were common problems and that I was more or less able to control them. But now I don't know. I feel like I'm just faking it.
<v Scott Carrier>直到现在，我还以为这些都是常见问题，我或多或少能控制住它们。但现在我不知道了。我觉得我只是在伪装。

00:46:57.030 --> 00:47:08.260
<v Scott Carrier>Eventually, late in the afternoon, I find the man, or at least I think he's the man. I'm a third of the way through the test before I realize he's not the right guy.
<v Scott Carrier>最终，傍晚时分，我找到了那个人，或者至少我觉得是那个人。测试做到三分之一时，我才意识到他不是我要找的人。

00:47:08.260 --> 00:47:17.050
<v Scott Carrier>When was your last visit to a mental health clinic? I don't go to a clinic. When did you last see a doctor? I don't have a doctor.
<v Scott Carrier>你上次去心理健康诊所是什么时候？我没去诊所。你上次看医生是什么时候？我没医生。

00:47:17.050 --> 00:47:24.340
<v Scott Carrier>Do you blame yourself for anything you've done or not done? No. Have you felt more self-confident than usual? No.
<v Scott Carrier>你对自己做过或没做过的事感到内疚吗？没有。你感觉比平时更自信吗？没有。

00:47:24.340 --> 00:47:44.220
<v Scott Carrier>Have you heard voices or other things that weren't there or that other people couldn't hear or seen things that weren't there? And he says, I think you want to talk to my son. And I ask him what his son's name is, and he says, same as mine.
<v Scott Carrier>你是否听到过不存在的声音或其他事物，或者是别人听不到的声音，或者是看到过不存在的东西？他说：“我觉得你想找我儿子谈谈。”我问他儿子叫什么，他说：“和我同名。”

00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:53.740
<v Scott Carrier>I come back the next morning and interview the son in the kitchen. They make coffee for me on a propane camp stove as the house has no electricity.
<v Scott Carrier>第二天早上我回来，在厨房里采访他儿子。他们在丙烷野营炉上为我煮咖啡，因为屋里没电。

00:47:53.740 --> 00:48:03.610
<v Scott Carrier>The son is 19 years old, a good looking kid, tall, healthy. Says used to run cross country in high school. He seems to be fine.
<v Scott Carrier>儿子19岁，长得很帅，个子高，健康。说高中时曾跑越野。他看起来没事。

00:48:03.610 --> 00:48:22.450
<v Scott Carrier>But as I go through the questions, he starts to fix his eyes on mine, a direct, almost hypnotic stare straight into my head, like he's trying to pull me in and trap me. I try to look back, to look just as deeply into his mind. But it's like looking into a cave.
<v Scott Carrier>但随着问题的进行，他开始盯着我的眼睛看，一种直接的、近乎催眠的直视，仿佛想把我吸进去困住。我试着回看，试图同样深地看进他的思想里。但这就像看着一个洞穴。

00:48:22.450 --> 00:48:38.030
<v Scott Carrier>He says he hears voices, satanic voices, and that he worries a lot about his shoes, that they're not the right kind, not the kind he sees on MTV. I can't tell if he's sick or if he's just trying to torture me.
<v Scott Carrier>他说他听到了声音，撒旦的声音，他很担心自己的鞋，说它们不对，不是他在MTV上看到的那种。我无法判断他是有病，还是只是想折磨我。

00:48:38.030 --> 00:49:05.010
<v Scott Carrier>And I drive away thinking I don't know anything about this disease, that I know even less than when I started. I spent two days driving around, and I made $30. And I feel really, really tired.
<v Scott Carrier>我开车离开时想，我对这种疾病一无所知，我知道的比开始时还要少。我花了两天时间开车绕路，只赚了30美元。我感到非常、非常累。

00:49:05.010 --> 00:49:23.570
<v Scott Carrier>The house is dark, as all the windows have heavy curtains pulled nearly shut. The curtains over the big picture window in the living room are open just a bit, and the light cuts through like a laser beam and hits the red shag carpet, throwing up small dust particles and cigarette ash.
<v Scott Carrier>屋里很暗，所有窗户都拉着厚重的窗帘。客厅大落地窗的窗帘开了一点，光像激光束一样切入，照在红色的毛毯上，激起微小的尘粒和香烟灰。

00:49:23.570 --> 00:49:44.320
<v Scott Carrier>Two feet away from the light, near the television, is a slice of pizza lying upside down in the carpet. I'm interviewing the woman-- a mother-- and her teenage daughter's on the phone talking to her boyfriend, or rather a series of boyfriends who call and call. And all of them want her to go out right now, but her mother won't let her.
<v Scott Carrier>离光线两英尺远，电视机旁，有一片披萨倒扣在毯子上。我正在采访那个女人——一个母亲——她的少女女儿正在打电话和男朋友聊天，或者说是一系列打电话的男朋友。他们都想让她现在出去，但她母亲不让。

00:49:44.320 --> 00:50:02.050
<v Scott Carrier>She's trying to answer my questions, trying to concentrate and be polite. But she's mainly listening to what her daughter is saying on the phone and will suddenly switch from saying, no, no, I've been feeling fine, I haven't had a relapse in months, to screaming out, is that John? I told you never to talk to him again.
<v Scott Carrier>她正试图回答我的问题，试图集中精神表现得礼貌。但她主要在听女儿电话里说的话，突然从说“不，不，我感觉一直很好，我已经几个月没复发了”转向尖叫道：“那是John吗？我告诉过你永远别再跟他说话了。”

00:50:02.050 --> 00:50:08.140
<v Scott Carrier>Or, who is it? Is it a boy? You can't go out. Tell him he has to come over here.
<v Scott Carrier>或者是：“是谁？是个男孩吗？你不能出去。告诉他，他必须来这儿。”

00:50:08.140 --> 00:50:25.860
<v Scott Carrier>I can't stop looking at the slice of pizza on the carpet. I keep looking at the slice of pizza because it's the only clue that the woman is sick. I mean, she has a teenage daughter and a dirty house. And maybe she shouldn't try to wear makeup to bed. But these are not necessarily symptoms of schizophrenia.
<v Scott Carrier>我忍不住盯着毯子上的那片披萨。我一直盯着那片披萨，因为那是那个女人有病的唯一线索。我是说，她有个少女女儿，房子很脏。也许她不该试着带妆睡觉。但这些未必是精神分裂症的症状。

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:41.090
<v Scott Carrier>She seems to be fine, just worn out, until I get to the question have you been worrying a lot? And she says yes, she has. She's been worrying a lot that the elders of the church-- the Mormon church-- will take her daughter away from her.
<v Scott Carrier>她看起来还好，只是累坏了，直到我问到“你最近担心很多吗？”她说是的。她一直很担心教会的长老——摩门教——会把她女儿从她身边带走。

00:50:41.090 --> 00:51:08.300
<v Scott Carrier>And I ask her why, and she says, because she stopped taking her medication. And I ask her why did you stop taking your medication? And she says that the only reason she takes it is because she told her bishop that she was visited by the Archangel Gabriel and that she'd had sex with him. And then she was also visited by the Archangel Michael and that she'd had sex with both of them at once and that they'd ravished her almost every night.
<v Scott Carrier>我问她为什么，她说，因为她停止服用药物了。我问她为什么停药？她说她吃药的唯一理由是她告诉过主教，大天使加百列曾造访她，并且她和他发生了性关系。然后大天使米迦勒也造访了她，她同时和他们两个发生了性关系，他们几乎每晚都蹂躏她。

00:51:08.300 --> 00:51:22.570
<v Scott Carrier>So her bishop made her go to a doctor. And the doctor gave her some pills, and she took the pills, and the angels stopped coming. The bishop and the elders had told her that if she had sex with any more angels, they'd take her daughter away.
<v Scott Carrier>所以主教强迫她去看医生。医生给了她一些药丸，她吃了药，天使们就不再来了。主教和长老们告诉她，如果她再和任何天使发生性关系，他们就会把她女儿带走。

00:51:22.570 --> 00:51:40.840
<v Scott Carrier>So I ask her again why she stopped taking her medication, and she says, I'm lonely. I miss them. I want them to come back.
<v Scott Carrier>所以我再问她为什么停药，她说：“我很孤独。我想念他们。我想要他们回来。”

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:54.030
<v Scott Carrier>Today in a restaurant, eating lunch between interviews, I decided to take the test. I answered the questions and scored myself appropriately. And at some point, I realized I wasn't doing so well.
<v Scott Carrier>今天在餐馆，在采访间隙吃午餐时，我决定做这个测试。我回答了问题，适当地给给自己评分。某一刻，我意识到我做得不怎么样。

00:51:54.030 --> 00:52:06.660
<v Scott Carrier>I decided not to even add up the points, because then I'd be left with a score and I'd never forget it. If I were to write a report on myself, it would sound something like this.
<v Scott Carrier>我决定甚至不去加总分数，因为那样我就有了一个分数，我永远也忘不掉。如果我要写一份关于我自己的报告，它听起来会是这样的。

00:52:06.660 --> 00:52:18.890
<v Scott Carrier>The patient is 36 years old and lives alone since his wife left him three weeks ago. She took the kids and all the silverware, except for a large knife and a bowl and a coffee cup.
<v Scott Carrier>患者36岁，自从三周前妻子离开后独自居住。她带走了孩子和所有的餐具，除了一把大刀、一个碗和一个咖啡杯。

00:52:18.890 --> 00:52:45.610
<v Scott Carrier>The patient admits that her leaving may have had something to do with the fact that, without warning, he completely gutted the house, tore out all the walls and ceilings, all the lathe and plaster right down to the studs. He says he did this in order to live like a primitive. When asked if he was successful, he says it was the first step in the right direction.
<v Scott Carrier>患者承认她离开可能与这一事实有关：毫无预兆地，他把房子彻底掏空了，拆掉了所有的墙壁和天花板，所有的板条和石膏板一直拆到龙骨。他说他这样做是为了像原始人一样生活。当被问及是否成功时，他说这是朝正确方向迈出的第一步。

00:52:45.610 --> 00:53:00.860
<v Scott Carrier>The patient is a 36-year-old male who lives alone since his wife and children left him two months ago. He says there's a darkness that separates him from other people, a heavy darkness, like looking at a person from the bottom of a well.
<v Scott Carrier>患者是一名36岁男性，自从两个月前妻子和孩子离开他后独自居住。他说有一道黑暗将他与其他人隔开，一道沉重的黑暗，就像从井底看着一个人。

00:53:00.860 --> 00:53:21.720
<v Scott Carrier>He believes that if he could say the right words, then the darkness would go away. He says he sometimes knows the right words, but cannot say them. Other times, he can't even think of the words to say.
<v Scott Carrier>他相信如果他能说出正确的词，黑暗就会消失。他说他有时知道正确的词，但说不出来。其他时候，他甚至连要说的词都想不出来。

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:34.990
<v Scott Carrier>The patient is 36 years old and lives alone since his wife and children left him three months ago. Last week, he went fishing in the San Juans and now believes that there's no better fisherman than himself.
<v Scott Carrier>患者36岁，自从三个月前妻子和孩子离开后独自居住。上周，他在圣胡安钓鱼，现在相信没有比他更出色的钓鱼者了。

00:53:34.990 --> 00:53:57.700
<v Scott Carrier>He says, I can't tell you about it, because talking about fishing is silly, like farting and tap dancing at the same time. All I can say is I walk around in the water and I know the instant the fish will jump for the fly. I cut open their stomachs and squeeze out the bugs in my hand, study what they eat, how it all gets digested, even the exoskeleton and wings.
<v Scott Carrier>他说：“我不能告诉你，因为谈论钓鱼很傻，就像同时放屁和踢踏舞一样。我只能说我在水里走来走去，我知道鱼儿跳起来咬钩的那一瞬间。我切开它们的肚子，把虫子挤在手里，研究它们吃什么，这一切是如何消化的，甚至是外骨骼和翅膀。”

00:53:57.700 --> 00:54:26.920
<v Scott Carrier>He says he was sick before, but now he's OK, and that it was the fly rod, just holding the rod in his hand, that cured him. His house is clean. The electricity is on. The walls have been sheet rocked and painted white. He says, I'll have to ask her, beg her, and maybe she'll come back.
<v Scott Carrier>他说他以前有病，但现在好了，是飞钓竿，仅仅是手里拿着竿子，治愈了他。他的屋子很干净。电通了。墙壁已经钉好板材并漆成了白色。他说：“我得求她，恳求她，也许她会回来。”

00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:31.030
<v Ira Glass>Scott Carrier. We first broadcast this story years ago.
<v Ira Glass>Scott Carrier。我们几年前首次播出了这个故事。

00:54:31.030 --> 00:54:51.860
<v Ira Glass>Scott recently has just started a new podcast. It's called Home of the Brave. If you've heard him on our radio show over the years, you know he is really like nobody else on the radio. He's just a complete original, an amazing writer, great interviewer in stories where he does interviews. I, and many of us in public radio are like, super fans, and so it's exciting to see him finally have his own show.
<v Ira Glass>Scott最近刚开始一个新的播客。叫《勇敢者的家》（Home of the Brave）。如果你多年来在我们的广播节目中听过他，你就知道他真的是电台里独一无二的人。他是一个彻底的原创，一个了不起的作家，在采访中非常棒。我，以及我们公共广播电台的许多人都非常喜欢他，所以看到他终于拥有了自己的节目真是太令人兴奋了。

00:54:51.860 --> 00:56:13.970
<v Ira Glass>Again the name, Home of the Brave. It's free. You can find it at homebrave.com or at the iTunes store or Stitcher, or like we say here on the show, wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>再说一次名字，《Home of the Brave》。它是免费的。你可以在homebrave.com或者iTunes商店或者Stitcher上找到它，或者就像我们在节目里说的，在任何你获取播客的地方。

